\description{
Fits the weighted (marginal structural) outcome model to the weighted clones, and estimates risks,
risk differences and risk ratios at each horizon, plus the hazard ratio (\code{"cox"}, the default), the
odds ratio (\code{"logistic"}), and the restricted mean survival and lost time (\code{"km"} and \code{"cox"}).
}
\details{
The model is fitted to the clone \code{segments} (time scale \code{t = day - riskStart + 1}, intervals
\verb{(startTime, stopTime]}, with the segment weight applying to risk-set membership and to events at
\code{stopTime}). The covariate is the target indicator (strategy 1). Effects are target versus
comparator.
\itemize{
\item \code{"cox"} (the default): the hazard ratio of a weighted Cox regression (Cyclops \code{cox_counting},
Breslow ties), with competing events treated as censoring (cause-specific hazard ratio), and, at
the horizons, exactly the rows of \code{"km"} on the same weighted clones: the weighted Kaplan-Meier
(or, with competing death, Aalen-Johansen) risks, risk differences, risk ratios and restricted
means. Risks from the Cox model itself (its Breslow baseline hazard) are not reported: they assume
proportional hazards, which a grace period violates by design (see below), so they are biased.
The hazard ratio is reported because meta-analysis and empirical calibration use it. It has
caveats of its own: when the hazards are not proportional it averages the effect over follow-up
in a way that depends on the follow-up and the censoring, and hazards among survivors have a
built-in selection bias (Hernan 2010; Stensrud and Hernan 2020).
\item \code{"logistic"}: weighted pooled logistic regression (Cyclops \code{lr}) on one row per clone and interval
of \code{timeBasis$intervalLength} days that the clone enters (intervals counted from \code{riskStart}),
with the weight that applies on the clone's last day in the interval (the weight of its last
segment in the interval), and covariates intercept, target indicator, time basis, and (if
\code{armByTimeInteraction}) their interaction. The weighted clones must be split on the same interval
grid, by \code{computeCensoringWeights(outcomeTimeBasis = timeBasis)}. With daily intervals (the
default) every row carries the exact weight of its day, so a saturated time basis per strategy
(an indicator basis with \code{armByTimeInteraction = TRUE}) gives the weighted Kaplan-Meier or
Aalen-Johansen risks. With an indicator basis, an interval (or, with the interaction, a strategy
and interval) with rows but no events has a hazard of 0, and one in which every row has the event
a hazard of 1, the limits of the maximum likelihood fit; their rows are left out of the fit, and the
coefficients that only they inform (whose estimates are infinite) are \code{NA}. Risks are
\code{1 - prod(1 - h)} without competing events;
with competing death, the outcome model is fitted to rows with no death in the interval, a death
model to all rows, and the risk is that of Young et al. (2020). The model is fitted to the
weighted numbers of rows with and without an event per strategy and interval, which have the
same likelihood as the rows. The risk at a horizon is read at the end of the last complete
interval on or before it, with a warning when the horizon does not end an interval; it is \code{NA}
(with a warning) when no interval is complete by the horizon or the horizon is beyond the
follow-up. Coefficients the data do not identify (for example the arm-by-time interaction of an
interval only one strategy enters) are \code{NA}, and so are the risks that need them.
\item \code{"km"}: weighted Kaplan-Meier (no competing events) or Aalen-Johansen (competing death) per
strategy.
\item \code{"fineGray"} (needs competing death): the subdistribution hazard ratio of a weighted Fine-Gray
regression, fitted as the weighted Cox regression above on rows in which a clone that dies at T
stays in the risk set after T, with weight w(T) G(s-) / G(T-) at times s > T (w(T) its weight at
death, G the censoring survival of its strategy, chosen by \code{fineGrayCensoring} in
\code{\link[=createFitOutcomeModelArgs]{createFitOutcomeModelArgs()}}), and, at the horizons, the Aalen-Johansen rows of \code{"km"}, as for
\code{"cox"}. Cyclops fits these rows without expanding them when it supports Fine-Gray rows
(\code{Cyclops::convertToCyclopsData(fineGrayCensoring = )}); with \code{robustVarianceEngine = "ccw"}, or an
older Cyclops, the rows after each death are built here. The robust variance treats the weights and G
as fixed.
}

The Cox and pooled logistic models, and their robust and model-based variances, use the weights
chosen by \code{fitOutcomeModelArgs$msmWeights} (unstabilized by default; see
\code{\link[=createFitOutcomeModelArgs]{createFitOutcomeModelArgs()}} for why): the segments' \code{weight} when it is on that scale, and their
\code{otherScaleWeight} otherwise (see \code{\link[=computeCensoringWeights]{computeCensoringWeights()}}). The Kaplan-Meier and Aalen-Johansen
estimates (of \code{"km"}, and the rows at the horizons of \code{"cox"}) use \code{weight}; they are the same with
either weight.

Horizons are days since index and are read at \code{t = horizon - riskStart + 1}, so the risk includes
events on the horizon day.

The \code{"logistic"} risks are model-based. Without \code{armByTimeInteraction} they assume an effect of the
strategy that is constant over follow-up (proportional odds). A grace period violates this
assumption by design: the clones of both strategies follow the same course during the grace period.
Their risks, risk differences and risk ratios are then biased, also at horizons within the grace
period, so with a grace period use \code{armByTimeInteraction = TRUE}. With a smooth time basis, such as
the default natural spline, the risks can remain biased even then, because each strategy's hazard
can change abruptly after the grace period, and with intervals longer than a day, clones that end
inside an interval count as at risk for the whole interval (see \code{\link[=createFitOutcomeModelArgs]{createFitOutcomeModelArgs()}}). The
\code{"km"} risks, and so the \code{"cox"} risks, do not make these assumptions. With a strategy whose grace
period ends on or after \code{riskStart}, \code{"logistic"} warns when the time basis is smooth (natural
spline, polynomial or linear), and when no interval ends at the end of the grace period, the end of
day \code{G} (\code{t = G - riskStart + 1}; day \code{G - 1} with \code{sameDayOrdering = "deviationFirst"}), where the
clones of an \code{"initiateWithin(G)"} strategy that have not started are censored. An indicator basis on
a grid with an interval ending there, with \code{armByTimeInteraction = TRUE}, avoids both.

The models are fitted with Cyclops using \code{fitOutcomeModelArgs$control}; a fit that fails is refitted
with Cyclops' default algorithm and convergence criterion, with a warning. When the Cox model cannot
be fitted (for example because there are no outcomes), the hazard ratio is \code{NA} and the \code{"km"} rows
are still reported. When the death model of the pooled logistic model with competing death cannot be
fitted, the odds ratio is still reported, and the risks are \code{NA}.

The robust variance (\code{varianceEstimator = "robust"}) is the HC0 sandwich clustered on person, so all
clones of a person share a cluster, treating the weights as fixed. For the hazard ratio and the
pooled logistic coefficients it is Cyclops' \code{vcov(fit, type = "robust")} by default, or this
package's own sandwich with \code{robustVarianceEngine = "ccw"} (see \code{\link[=createFitOutcomeModelArgs]{createFitOutcomeModelArgs()}}). For
\code{"cox"} and \code{"km"} the standard errors of the risks, their differences and ratios, and the restricted
means are the infinitesimal-jackknife standard errors of the weighted Kaplan-Meier or Aalen-Johansen
estimates; for \code{"logistic"} they use the delta method with a numerical Jacobian. With
\code{varianceEstimator = "modelBased"} the hazard ratio or odds ratio uses the inverse of the weighted
information; the \code{"cox"} rows at the horizons then have no standard errors or limits (the
Kaplan-Meier has no model-based variance), and \code{"km"} is not supported. \code{varianceEstimator = "bootstrap"} is computed by \code{\link[=bootstrapCcwAnalysis]{bootstrapCcwAnalysis()}}, not by this function.

\code{varianceEstimator = "stackedSandwich"} (an option; \code{"robust"} stays the default) stacks the
estimating equations of the outcome estimates with those of the censoring models and their
stabilization numerators, so that the variance accounts for the estimation of the weights (Shu et al.
2021). Person i's influence is its infinitesimal-jackknife influence (that of this package's own
robust sandwich, whatever \code{robustVarianceEngine} says) plus \verb{psi_i' A^- D}, summed over the censoring
models, where \code{psi_i} is the person's centered score in the model (summed over the person's entries
when a person has several index dates), \code{A} the model's information (with
a pivoted-Cholesky generalized inverse) and \code{D} the derivative of the estimates with respect to the
model's coefficients through the weights. The scores and the information come from the censoring
models' stored predictions and the covariates of \code{cloneCensorWeightData}, for the persons of each
model's risk set (which can differ from the outcome population when the models are shared across
outcomes). It supports the binary models at \code{G} and the pooled logistic hazard models of every
strategy type and of every \code{initiateWithinModel}, \code{postGraceModel}, \code{graceRegime} and \code{graceStart},
with stabilized or unstabilized weights. The derivative of each log weight is taken on the scale the
estimates use (\code{weight} for the Kaplan-Meier and Aalen-Johansen rows, and \code{otherScaleWeight} for the
Cox and pooled logistic models when \code{msmWeights} is of the other kind, truncated on its own scale or
formed from the truncated \code{weight} as \code{truncateMsmWeights} says), holding the truncation caps fixed: a
truncated weight depends on the censoring models only through its numerator, if at all. This is exact
for fixed caps and conditional on the cap for percentile caps. A normal prior is taken at its variance
(its curvature \code{s_j^2 / sigma^2}, with \code{s_j} the covariate's largest value over the model's risk set,
the normalization of the covariates, is added to \code{A}); as in the fit, the interactions of a
covariate that the prior excludes are penalized. A Laplace prior is taken conditional on its
support (the nonzero coefficients) and on its variance, which ignores the selection of the covariates
and of the variance by cross-validation. A hazard model fit on a uniform sample of its risk set
(\code{maxCohortSizeForFitting}) has scores for the persons of the sample only. It stops for the Cox
censoring model, for binary models that \code{\link[CohortMethod:createPs]{CohortMethod::createPs()}} fit on a sample (set
\code{maxCohortSizeForFitting = 0} or use the bootstrap), for indicator intervals whose hazard a sample
left to a plug-in from the whole risk set, and for censoring models saved by an earlier version, which
must be refit.
}
\examples{
# Simulated data, its study population, and the clones of two strategies:
data <- simulateCloneCensorWeightData(n = 200, maxDays = 60, graceEnd = 7, seed = 1)
population <- createStudyPopulation(
  cloneCensorWeightData = data,
  outcomeId = 3,
  createStudyPopulationArgs = CohortMethod::createCreateStudyPopulationArgs(
    riskWindowEnd = 60,
    endAnchor = "cohort start"
  )
)
clones <- createClones(
  population = population,
  cloneCensorWeightData = data,
  createClonesArgs = createCreateClonesArgs(
    strategies = list(
      createTreatmentStrategy(1, "Initiate within 7 days", "initiateWithin", graceEnd = 7),
      createTreatmentStrategy(2, "Do not initiate within 7 days", "notInitiateWithin", graceEnd = 7)
    )
  )
)
# The censoring models (without the baseline covariates, to keep the example fast; by default they
# use all covariates) and the weighted clones, also split on the daily grid of the pooled logistic
# outcome model:
censoringModels <- fitCensoringModels(
  clones = clones,
  cloneCensorWeightData = data,
  fitCensoringModelsArgs = createFitCensoringModelsArgs(useCovariates = FALSE)
)
weightedClones <- computeCensoringWeights(
  clones = clones,
  censoringModels = censoringModels,
  computeWeightsArgs = createComputeWeightsArgs(),
  outcomeTimeBasis = createTimeBasis(type = "naturalSpline", intervalLength = 1, df = 4)
)
outcomeModel <- fitOutcomeModel(
  weightedClones = weightedClones,
  fitOutcomeModelArgs = createFitOutcomeModelArgs(modelType = "km", horizons = c(30, 60))
)
outcomeModel
confint(outcomeModel)
# The pooled logistic model with a daily indicator basis and strategy-by-time interactions, which
# follow the change in the hazards at the end of the grace period:
logisticModel <- fitOutcomeModel(
  weightedClones = weightedClones,
  fitOutcomeModelArgs = createFitOutcomeModelArgs(
    modelType = "logistic",
    horizons = c(30, 60),
    timeBasis = createTimeBasis(type = "indicator", intervalLength = 1),
    armByTimeInteraction = TRUE
  )
)
coef(logisticModel)
close(weightedClones)
close(clones)
close(data)

}
